// expect-error: pin(T1) cannot hold: this value is T2
// expect-error: inference converged, so this is a proven-false assertion
//
// SPEC 5.4.1, and SPEC 5.0.1's decision made visible: an annotation is an
// assertion the compiler verifies and may refuse, not a directive it carries out.
//
// `out` demonstrably escapes -- it is returned -- so no arena can hold it and T1
// is false. The analysis converged, so this is a wrong claim about the
// programmer's own program rather than a limit of the analysis, and SPEC 5.4.1
// separates those two cases precisely so that this one can be an error without
// touching SPEC 1's invariant.
//
// The contrast that makes the rule legible: an *unproven* pin (budget exhausted)
// warns and is ignored (SPEC 5.4.2), and a wrong *axiom* -- `unique`, `region` --
// warns and is discarded (SPEC 5). Only the constraint fails the build, because
// only the constraint has nothing to fall back to: discarding a refuted `pin`
// silently returns the value to the tier the programmer just said was
// unacceptable.

struct Node {
    id: Int,
    weight: Int
}

fn leaks(i: Int) -> Node {
    let pin(T1) out = Node { id: i, weight: i }
    return out
}

fn main() -> Int {
    let n = leaks(4)
    return n.id
}
